==================== Input ====================
# A mapping is permuted while a comment is still pending further down the stream.
#
# This shape is why the permuted separator's assert can only claim that a pending comment starts at or
# beyond the end of the mapping being printed. It cannot claim the gap it is handed is forward-ordered:
# the entries have moved, so the "previous" entry can sit later in the source than the next one, and a
# comment pass measuring that gap sees an inverted range.
#
# An earlier assert here demanded either a forward gap or no pending comment at all, which a document
# as ordinary as this one violates -- it crashed debug builds while release was perfectly correct. It
# was covered only incidentally, as a side effect of another fixture, so it is named here.
#
# `may_reorder` is what makes the weaker claim true: a comment anywhere in the mapping's own printing
# scope refuses the mapping outright, so any comment that survives to be pending is genuinely below it.
#
# `get` must come out reordered below, or this fixture proves nothing: a refused mapping never reaches
# the permuted separator. The `info` mapping is what keeps the tail comment out of `get`'s trivia scope
# -- without it the comment is adjacent to `get` and refuses it, which is a different fixture.
#
# The root is refused, though, which is why `info` stays after `paths` rather than moving up behind
# `openapi`: the tail comment is in the root's trivia scope. Delete that comment and the root reorders.
# So the two facts this fixture pins are one and the same refusal, applied per mapping.
openapi: 3.0.0
paths:
  /p:
    get:
      responses: {}
      summary: s
      operationId: op
info:
  version: "1"
  title: t

# Still pending while `get` above is permuted, and belongs to nothing that moved.

